w, and lacks a central mandate. I'll address the second claim head-on: the lack of a global plan. I'll explain *why* there isn't one. The decentralized nature of the Internet is the crux of the matter. There's no "Internet President" calling the shots. Then, I'll provide nuance around the first claim – the stalled rollout. I’ll make it clear that it hasn't stopped, but the growth curve has flattened. I'll explain the reasons: the "long tail" problem, the "Band-aid" effect of CGNAT (Carrier-Grade NAT) still being "good enough" for many, and enterprise inertia. I’ll also make sure to highlight the economic drivers, because this is the real force. Since no one can force the switch, economics will. IPv4 addresses are getting expensive, as AWS recently started charging for public IPv4 addresses. That’s a massive market signal that’s pushing the industry towards IPv6. My conclusion will be that the article the user read makes valid points about the lack of a mandate and the sluggishness of the IPv6 rollout, but total stagnation isn't accurate. It's more of a slow, economically-driven grind rather than a coordinated global flip of a switch. I’ll be careful about my language and make sure not to appear combative. I'll phrase it as something like, "While you didn't explicitly state a position in your first question, the sentiment that the IPv6 transition is a slow, uncoordinated mess is absolutely correct." I'll also bring in recent data points like AWS charging for IPv4 and CGNAT to back up the points. Finally, I’ll organize all this with clear headings or bullet points. It's all about providing an informative, objective, and conversational tone.
21:09:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a very fair point. While you didn't explicitly state a stance in your first question, if your underlying thought is that **the transition to IPv6 is a slow, uncoordinated, and somewhat messy process**, that article is absolutely right—and it does back up that sentiment. Here is why the article you read is largely correct, along with some context on where we actually stand: ### 1. True: There is no unified global plan to force the switch The internet is fundamentally decentralized. There is no "President of the Internet," and organizations like ICANN or IANA only manage the *distribution* of addresses, not the hardware that runs them. Because of this, no single authority can force an ISP in rural America, a bank in London, or a local government in Japan to spend thousands of dollars upgrading their routers, firewalls, and software to support IPv6. The transition is entirely voluntary and market-driven. ### 2. Mostly True: The rollout has "stalled" (or rather, plateaued) Saying it has completely stalled might be a slight exaggeration, but the *rate of growth* has absolutely slowed down. If you look at Google’s IPv6 adoption tracker, global adoption grew rapidly from about 10% in 2016 to 35% in 2021. But since then, the growth curve has flattened out. Today, we are sitting at about 40% to 45% global adoption. **Why has it slowed down?** * **The easy work is done:** The major players—like mobile networks (T-Mobile, Verizon), massive ISPs (Comcast), and tech giants (Google, Meta, Netflix)—have already made the switch. For example, if you are reading this on a smartphone using cellular data, you are almost certainly using IPv6 right now. * **The "Long Tail" problem:** The remaining 55% of the internet consists of millions of smaller businesses, legacy enterprise networks, local ISPs, and older websites. For them, upgrading to IPv6 is expensive, time-consuming, and offers no immediate return on investment. * **The band-aids worked too well:** Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT) and the secondary market for buying IPv4 addresses (mentioned in the previous answer) were meant to be temporary bridges. Instead, they worked so well that many companies just decided to stick with IPv4 indefinitely. ### So, how does the switch actually happen if no one is forcing it? Since there is no global mandate, the final push to IPv6 will be driven purely by **economics**. We are actually seeing a major shift happen right now. In February 2024, **Amazon Web Services (AWS)**—which hosts a massive chunk of the internet—started charging its customers a new fee for every public IPv4 address they use. (Before this, they were included for free). When the biggest cloud provider in the world starts hitting companies in their wallets for clinging to IPv4, those companies suddenly have a financial incentive to migrate to IPv6, which remains free. Other cloud providers and server hosts are expected to follow suit. ### The Bottom Line The article you read is spot on about the lack of a global mandate and the sluggishness of the current rollout. We will likely be living in a "Dual Stack" world (where both IPv4 and IPv6 run simultaneously) for another decade or more. The "death" of IPv4 won't be a sudden shutdown; it will be a slow, expensive fade into irrelevance.
